   "prompt": "Bug: pickJobRoute in tools/swarm/core-41.mjs (about line 463) computes lane pass-rate failures with passRateFailuresFor(options.root ?? process.cwd()), and most callers do not pass options.root, so routing reads the run history of whatever directory the process was started in instead of the project being routed. Symptoms: (1) tests that route jobs in a temp project (tests/escalation-routing.test.mjs, tests/lanes-run.test.mjs, tests/router*.test.mjs and others, about 6 tests) fail whenever the checkout they run from has real failed runs under its own .swarm/runs (for example the wide-wave checkout after a few failed Claude jobs: 'route-exhausted: no lane left for job (tier expensive)'), which breaks the local merge-queue test gate; (2) in production, an orchestrator whose cwd is not the project root routes with the wrong project's pass rates. Fix: every caller of pickJobRoute (validateProject, runManifest, the ticket manifest builder and anything else found with grep) must pass the project root it is working on as options.root; make options.root required in practice by defaulting only when truly absent, and never fall back to process.cwd() when a root is known. Add tests in tests/pass-rate-project-root.test.mjs: with cwd set to a temp directory that holds failing run states for the claude lane (create .swarm/runs/<id>/state.json fixtures the way tests/lane-pass-routing.test.mjs does) and a different temp project root without runs, validateProject routes a tier-expensive job to claude (not exhausted); with the failing run states in the project root itself, it is exhausted as before. Run tests/escalation-routing.test.mjs, tests/lanes-run.test.mjs, tests/lane-pass-routing*.test.mjs, tests/router*.test.mjs and tests/api-workers.test.mjs from a cwd that has failing claude runs under .swarm/runs and show they pass.",
